  note    -

dropped
  go      Int.GobEncode, Int.GobDecode, Rat.GobEncode, Rat.GobDecode: Go's own binary serialisation; an integer is written as bytes or text by the blocks above
  go      Int.Format, Int.Scan, Rat.Scan's fmt.State and fmt.ScanState: the machinery of Go's fmt interfaces, what they select being in the blocks above
  go      Int.Bits, Int.SetBits, big.Word: the raw words of Go's representation, unchecked; an integer's bits are read by `bit` and its bytes by `to_bytes`
  go      big.Int's receiver-as-result form (z.Add(x, y) writing into z): an allocation hint; every function here answers a new value
